<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e>
      a       zoo:Oeuvre ;
      zoo:author "ANTIGONUS" ;
      zoo:date "date not found" ;
      zoo:editor "Unknown editor" ;
      zoo:file "./zoo/zoo4/2e.xml" ;
      zoo:identifier "collection_of_marvellous_accounts" ;
      zoo:title "Collection of Marvellous Accounts" .


<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/63/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/63> ;
      zoo:text "The ass and the aigithos, too, are at war with one another: for the ass comes by and scratches itself on thorns and because of this, especially whenever it brays, it throws out the eggs of the aigithos and the nestlings fall out in fright. Because of this injury, the aigithos flies at it and pecks its sores." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/35/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/35> ;
      zoo:text "He says that does give birth by the roadside, to avoid predatory animals, for wolves are least likely to attack them there. They also lead their offspring to their lair, so as to accustom them to their place of refuge – this is a precipitous rock with only one exit. A female deer had previously been captured which had ivy on its antlers as though they were moist. Deer are captured by piping and singing so that they lie down, overcome with pleasure." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/67/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/67> ;
      zoo:text "He says that all the land animals that have lungs breathe but that wasps and bees do not breathe." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/31/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/31> ;
      zoo:text "Aristotle says that the animal called the elk undergoes this as well: it is a quadruped almost equal in size to the ass, thick-skinned and hairy, and he says that it is remarkable how its hair changes colour so rapidly." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/99/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/99> ;
      zoo:text "Octopi rule over crayfish, for they suffer no injury from the latter’s shells. And over octopi, the conger eels, for the octopi are unable to deal with them because of their smoothness. And over the conger, the crayfish: for the conger does not glance off the crayfish but is lacerated by the jaggedness of its shell." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/76/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/76> ;
      zoo:text "Of deer, the so-called achaïnai seem to have gall in the tail." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/39/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/39> ;
      zoo:text "He says that the Egyptian plover cleans out the teeth of crocodiles and gains its nourishment from them. The crocodile perceives it as useful and whenever the trochilos wants to get out, the crocodile moves its neck so that the bird is not bitten by the closing teeth." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/26/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/26> ;
      zoo:text "The bat alone of birds has teeth and breasts and milk. Aristotle says that the seal, too, and the whale have milk. And he writes down something no less prodigious than these, for he says that in Lemnos milk was extracted from a he-goat in sufficient quantities to make cheese." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/112/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/112> ;
      zoo:text "Laconian dogs copulate better when tired out." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/7/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/7> ;
      zoo:text "There is something singular, too, about the guts of sheep: those of the ram are soundless, while those of the ewe are melodious. One might understand the Poet – who has a real thirst for knowledge and is exceedingly learned – to be referring to this when he says: he strung seven strings of female sheep" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/41/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/41> ;
      zoo:text "The weasel, whenever it fights with a snake, eats mountain rue beforehand, for its smell is repugnant to the snake. In addition, rue helps against snake-bite when it is placed in unmixed wine and drunk. And a pig, when it is bitten by a snake, takes itself at once to rivers and seeks the crab: this, too, has been recorded and is extremely efficacious for snake-bite." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/107/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/107> ;
      zoo:text "While the hawk bears three ⟨eggs, it hatches only two⟩, and when the young have grown it selects one, for it is not able to feed both because throughout this time its talons are distorted and it cannot seize anything. The vulture takes over the care of the one left to wander." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/89/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/89> ;
      zoo:text "And the sponge is capable of perception: if it becomes aware that someone is about to pull it away, it contracts and is a hard job to remove. The same thing happens when there is wind or a rough sea." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/72/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/72> ;
      zoo:text "Among the Illyrians and in Paeonia there is a ⟨pig⟩ with uncloven feet. No two-horned animal has been found with uncloven hooves, and ⟨few⟩ one-horned beasts with uncloven hoof, like the Indian ass (this animal alone of the uncloven-footed has a knucklebone, too)." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/58/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/58> ;
      zoo:text "They say that the bison is found in Paeonia on the mountain of Marsanos and that it has no upper teeth, like the ox or any other two-horned beast, and in other respects is similar to the bull. When it is pursued, it projects its excrement quite a distance; whenever it does this in fright, its dung burns in such a way that the hounds’ hair falls out, but if it does it free from fear, nothing suffers or is injured." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/44/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/44> ;
      zoo:text "Pigeons do not like to have multiple mating partners nor to leave their pairing unless they are widowed. For their young, they chew up salty earth and spit the food which they have prepared into their mouths." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/29/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/29> ;
      zoo:text "Another cause for amazement are those animals which, like the octopus, make themselves similar to their surrounding: for it becomes indistinguishable from the colour of the sea-bed and from that of anything which it might entwine, so that it is difficult to catch. It is clearly from this fact that the poet wrote the oft-quoted verse: Having the spirit of the octopus in your breast, child, adapt yourself to ⟨whatever is in accordance with popular sentiment⟩." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/13/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/13> ;
      zoo:text "In neither the Scythian region nor, likewise, that of Elis, are any mules born." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/4/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/4> ;
      zoo:text "Nor do the frogs give voice in Seriphus: and a similar legend also prevails among the Seriphians, except that while the Rhegines tell it about Heracles, the Seriphians tell it about Perseus." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/53/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/53> ;
      zoo:text "The electric ray hides itself in the sand and seizes the fish that, whenever they come near, are rendered incapable of swimming because of the torpor it induces." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/77/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/77> ;
      zoo:text "Fish do not have a gullet: because of this, the stomach of larger fish falls forward into their mouths when they chase smaller ones." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/17/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/17> ;
      zoo:text "Myrsilus of Lesbos says that on Mt. Lepetymnus there is a sanctuary of Apollo and a heroön of Lepetymnus, on which, just as in Crannon, there are only two ravens, although there are not a few in the nearby areas." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/103/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/103> ;
      zoo:text "With animals, the larger are male and the lesser, female." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/48/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/48> ;
      zoo:text "It is said by some that no-one has seen either the young of the vulture or its nest. And because of this, Herodorus, the father of Bryson the sophist, says that they are from some foreign high land, for they lay their eggs on inaccessible rocks." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/66/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/66> ;
      zoo:text "Lycus narrates something similar to this. He says that in Libya the flocks generally sleep with some facing one another while others sleep in whatever way they chance to lie down, but on the night on which the dog-star rises, they are all turned facing that same star. The inhabitants use this as evidence of its rising. Aristotle also goes through other such matters, apart from the instincts of animals concerning their way of life, using a great deal of care for the most part, since he is concerned with a major task and not a by-work in his explanation of them. In total, he has written nearly seventy books on these matters, and has tried to dwell more on explanation than on narrative in each. As regards my excerpt, it is sufficient for it to summarize the strange and paradoxical content of both the aforementioned and other writings." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/92/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/92> ;
      zoo:text "He says that around the river Hypanis in the Cimmerian Bosphorus, about the Summer solstice, things like pouches, larger than grapes, are carried down, from which when they break open a four-footed winged animal emerges that lives for one day: it is singular as a winged creature in being four-legged." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/36/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/36> ;
      zoo:text "The wild goats in Crete, whenever they are struck by an arrow, seek dittany, for it seems to be effective in casting out arrows." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/19/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/19> ;
      zoo:text "Of the Libyans, there are some called Psylli, with whom something happens in the opposite fashion to this: for they suffer nothing when struck by asps, but of the other Libyans, not one can escape once bitten." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/100/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/100> ;
      zoo:text "He says that the mullet, whenever it is afraid, hides its head, as if it were hiding the whole of its body." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/62/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/62> ;
      zoo:text "The little owl and the crow are enemies: while the crow ⟨steals away the eggs⟩ of the owl by day because the owl cannot see, the owl does the same to the crow by night when the crow cannot see. So while one rules by night, the other does by day." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/75/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/75> ;
      zoo:text "The heart of the horse is bony, as is that of some cattle." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/113/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/113> ;
      zoo:text "The procreation of mice is amazing because of its speed: a pregnant female once got trapped in a jar and after a short time one hundred and twenty mice came to light. In some ⟨places⟩ in Persia, when the female mouse is dissected the embryos are discovered to be already pregnant." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/25/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/25> ;
      zoo:text "The octopus eats its own tentacles in Winter. As the poem has it, On a wintry day, when the boneless one gnaws its foot. The new-born of dog-fish come out from the belly and feed, and re-enter into the mouth. The lioness does not conceive twice: for, as Herodotus says, she ejects the womb with the new-born. Nor does the viper, for the emerging young eat through its belly." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/6/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/6> ;
      zoo:text "Our genre of excerption would include the partridges spoken of in Attica and Boeotia, of whom some are agreed to be melodious, others completely weak-voiced." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/108/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/108> ;
      zoo:text "In practically all cases, the crooked-clawed birds eject their young as soon as they are able to fly, except the crow which even when they can fly feeds them for some time." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/57/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/57> ;
      zoo:text "When bees have been fumigated and are badly affected by the smoke, it is then most of all that they eat ⟨honey⟩, but for the rest of the time they use it sparingly so as to store it by as food. They smear the hive with drops from the trees as a protection against other animals. When the worker bees kill other bees, they try to do it outside: if they kill within the hive, they carry out the body. The so-called ‘robber’ bees do damage if they come in unnoticed. But they seldom enter – for they are watched out for and guards are placed everywhere. There are bees appointed to each of the tasks: some gather nectar, others smooth the combs. They are disgusted both by the bad smell of food and by perfume, and go some distance from the hive to discharge their excrement. And the elders work inside ⟨ . . . . . ⟩ If one takes a wasp by the legs and lets its wings buzz, Aristotle says that stingless wasps fly towards it but none of those with stings do so." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/87/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/87> ;
      zoo:text "With partridges, if the female stands downwind of the male, it becomes fertilized." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/121/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/121> ;
      zoo:text "There are those born from lame or blind parents who are likewise lame or blind; there are some cases where even marks have been inherited." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/3/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/3> ;
      zoo:text "In Cephallenia, too, a river partitions the land, and while on the near side there are cicadas, there are none on the far side." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/12/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/12> ;
      zoo:text "Amelesagoras the Athenian, who wrote the Atthis, says that the crow does not fly towards the acropolis, and there is nobody who can say that they have seen one do so. He gives the reason in the form of a myth. For he says that when Athena was given to Hephaestus, as she lay down with him she vanished and Hephaestus fell to the ground and emitted his sperm. The earth, says Amelesagoras, afterwards bore to him Erichthonius, whom Athena reared and then shut up in a basket and handed over to Agraulos, Pandrosos and Herse, the daughters of Cecrops. Athena instructed them not to open the chest until she herself came back. When she went to Pellene to fetch a mountain so that she might make a defence in front of the acropolis, two of the daughters of Cecrops, Agraulos and Pandrosos, opened the chest and saw two serpents around Erichthonius. Amelesagoras says that a crow met Athena as she carried the mountain, which is now called Lycabettus, and told her that Erichthonius was out in the open. She, on hearing this, flung the mountain to its present position and said to the crow that because of the bad news it had brought, it was no longer lawful for it to approach the acropolis." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/54/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/54> ;
      zoo:text "The so-called ‘foxes’, whenever they sense that they have swallowed a fish-hook, run back and bite off the line from above it." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/28/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/28> ;
      zoo:text "The Poet, too, is said by everyone to be pretty painstaking and full of curiosity, for Odysseus, when the dogs had attacked him on the way up to the swineherd, crouched in cunning, and cast the staff from his hand. For they say that whenever anyone who is being chased crouches down, the dogs do him no harm." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/45/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/45> ;
      zoo:text "Partridges, whenever anyone hunts their young, roll about in front of the hunter as though they are disabled and draw them on, until the young escape. Because of their passion for sex, the males destroy the eggs so that the female does not incubate them; but she plots against this and runs off elsewhere to lay them. The 'widowers' fight among themselves and the one which is worsted follows the victor and is mounted by him alone." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/83/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/83> ;
      zoo:text "With some people, when they are sick their hair becomes grey, but when healthy grows black again." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/21/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/21> ;
      zoo:text "There is an island near the regions of Carystia and Andria which is called Gyaros; there, the mice eat through iron. In the island of Ceos, the wild pear is lethal, and if you were to graft it onto another tree, it would cause it to wither." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/78/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/78> ;
      zoo:text "Snakes have thirty ribs. And if one pricks their eyes out, they grow back again, just like those of the swallow." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/49/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/49> ;
      zoo:text "Some say, too, that 'cinnamon' refers to a bird and that it carries the spice and makes its nest from this. It nests in high and unclimbable trees, and the locals tip their arrows with lead to shoot at and break up the nests." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/50/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/50> ;
      zoo:text "The cuckoo is thought to sneak its young [into other birds' nests] because of its cowardice and inability to defend them, for cuckoos have their feathers plucked by the least of birds. Those birds that take in the young throw out their own because the cuckoo’s are so beautiful." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/33/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/33> ;
      zoo:text "He says that around Conopeium near Lake Maeotis the wolves procure their sustenance from the fishermen and guard their catch in return; but if they suspect that they have been cheated in any way, they ruin the nets and the fish." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/80/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/80> ;
      zoo:text "The bones of the lion are so hard that when they are struck together they frequently light fire." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/37/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/37> ;
      zoo:text "Some say that the leopard has been observed – since living things delight in its scent – to hide itself and thus prey on animals which come close to it." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/101/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/101> ;
      zoo:text "All birds of prey are given to mimicry." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/9/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/9> ;
      zoo:text "No partridges are to be found in the islands of the Lemnians called Neae: if anyone tries to introduce them, they die. Some say something even more prodigious than this – that they die if they even see the land." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/69/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/69> ;
      zoo:text "While many animals are bloodless, on the whole this is true for those that have more than four feet." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/61/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/61> ;
      zoo:text "Concerning the parturition of wolves, Aristotle makes a relation of something thoroughly fabulous (though he does so as one who is conscious of this): for he says that they all give birth in a single twelve-⟨day⟩ period of the year. The reason for this, as the story goes, is that they brought Leto from the Hyperboreans to Delos in twelve days, during which time she was in the form of a wolf." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/24/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/24> ;
      zoo:text "No less amazing than these examples is the destruction of what is useful. For example, the gecko, whenever it sheds its skin, turns around and swallows it: for it is, they say (as Aristotle writes), a cure for epilepsy. Likewise, the seal is said to disgorge its whey – this, indeed, is useful for the same disease. Mares eat off the excrescence on their newly born young called 'hippomanes': this is situated on the forehead and is sought for many purposes. The doe buries her right antler in the earth: this, too, is useful in many ways. But these [behaviours] demand a great deal of attention as to whether they are attributable to deliberate choice or to chance." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/110/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/110> ;
      zoo:text "Partridges build ⟨two⟩ compartments in the nest for their eggs and each of them [i.e. male and female] incubates and rears the chicks, and they copulate with the young when they first lead them out." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/74/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/74> ;
      zoo:text "The male has more teeth than the female in both humans and other animals." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/56/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/56> ;
      zoo:text "See, too, how the nautilus octopus is also unusual in what it does, for it has a shell which it turns downward on ascending so that it might more easily be propelled with it empty, but when it descends from above, it reverses the shell. It has a degree of webbing like a membrane between its tentacles up to a certain point and this, whenever there is a breeze, it uses as a sail; and instead of steering-oars lets down ⟨two⟩ of its tentacles alongside." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/42/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/42> ;
      zoo:text "The ring-dove, whenever it is struck, packs oregano into the wound and regains its health thereby." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/11/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/11> ;
      zoo:text "There are no snakes in Astypalaea, nor are there hares in Ithaca, nor the wild boar nor deer in Libya, nor the weasel in Rheneia which is near Delos; and the guinea-fowl is not to be seen at all in any place ⟨other than in Leros⟩." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/27/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/27> ;
      zoo:text "The male of the halcyon are called 'keryloi': when they become weak through old age and are no longer able to fly, the females carry them by taking them upon their wings. The saying of Alcman agrees with this, for he says that he is weak from old age and unable to go around with the choruses or with the dances of the maidens: No longer, maidens of honeyed speech, divine-voiced, are my limbs able to carry me: would, oh would that I were a kerylos, which over the foam of the wave flies with the halcyons with resolute heart – the sea-purple bird of Spring." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/10/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/10> ;
      zoo:text "Boeotia has a great many mole-rats: in Coroneia alone this animal is not to be found, but dies if it is introduced. The same is true of ⟨wolves and⟩ little owls in Crete, where they say that the region will not endure the presence of any deadly animal." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/84/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/84> ;
      zoo:text "If sheep drink from the river called Psychros in the Chalcidice, by Thrace, it causes their offspring to be black. And in Antandria there are two rivers, of which one makes offspring white, the other, black. The Scamander is supposed to make them yellow, and because of this the Poet addressed it as “Xanthus” [i.e., 'yellow'] instead of “Scamandrus.” And in Euboea, along the Histiaean boundary with Chalcis, there are two rivers, Ceron and Neleus: if goats drink about the time of conception from the Ceron, they give birth to black offspring but if from the Neleus, to white." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/105/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/105> ;
      zoo:text "In Syracuse, a drunkard buried eggs in the ground and put a rush-mat over them and drank continuously until such time as the eggs hatched. Eggs placed in warm vessels have actually been hatched and come forth." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/15/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/15> ;
      zoo:text "In Crannon in Thessaly they say that there are only two ravens: and because of this, on the city emblem attached to records of proxeny – as it is customary everwhere to attach the city emblem to such records – are depicted two ravens on a bronze cart, because more than this have never been seen. The cart is depicted for the following reason (and this, too, might seem outlandish): they have a bronze cart as a dedication which, whenever there is a drought, they shake while beseeching the god for water, and they say that their prayers are fulfilled. Theopompus tells something more singular than this: for he says that while the ravens stay in Crannon until such time as they hatch their young, once they have done this, they abandon the young and go back." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/2/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/2> ;
      zoo:text "Something else of the same sort is told as a legend by the Rhegines: that Heracles, when he was disturbed by cicadas after falling asleep at some spot in the region, prayed that they would become voiceless." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/51/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/51> ;
      zoo:text "The aigithos flies to the female goat and suckles her: it has taken its name from this fact. Whenever it suckles, the udder ⟨dries out and the goat⟩ goes blind. The bird is lame, from which Callimachus of Egypt, wishing to be very clear, said (having spoken previously of some other bird) “αἴγιθος ἀμφιγυήεις.” His account is not confirmed, for it is not lame in both feet. “ἀμφιγυήεις” means not lameness of the type in question, but that which is spoken of in connexion with Hephaestus – when one has been lamed in both limbs. I have been led to speak of Callimachus because of his implausibility." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/117/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/117> ;
      zoo:text "No eunuch becomes bald." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/20/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/20> ;
      zoo:text "In Lycia there grows a plant called goat's bane, which none of the local goats will taste, but whenever a foreign goat comes upon it and eats the plant through ignorance, it dies as a result of the destruction of that compartment of its stomach called the 'echinus'." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/96/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/96> ;
      zoo:text "This, too, is singular, that when the spinal cord of certain corpses rots, small snakes are generated from the spine, if the person breathed in the odour of a dead snake before death. We have encountered an epigram of Archelaus, of whom I have formerly made mention, who records this too concerning marvels and says: All beings through each other does a lifetime set its seal upon; From the cord of the hollow spine of a man Comes a terrible serpent, when the worthless body has rotted; Which takes new breath from this prodigy, Dragging living being from the dead: if this is so, The birth of dual-formed Cecrops is no marvel. So we make an established matter of this phenomenon, which is averred cursorily in hearsay and certain sayings in common currency, through the evidence of the epigram." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/79/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/79> ;
      zoo:text "Of the fish, the parrot-wrasse is the only one which ruminates." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/65/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/65> ;
      zoo:text "He says that the goatherds say that when the sun turns most quickly, the goats lie down facing it." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/34/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/34> ;
      zoo:text "In Thrace, in the city once called Cedripolis, men and hawks jointly hunt small birds: the former beat the trees while the hawks pursue closely and the little birds fall towards the men as they flee. Because of this, they share their prey with the hawks." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/115/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/115> ;
      zoo:text "[Whenever a she-goat] touches the tip of the beard – which is like hair – the others stand as if stupefied and stare at her." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/94/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/94> ;
      zoo:text "Land scorpions are killed by their children. The venom-spider, too, kills its female parent and often the male as well, for they incubate the eggs jointly." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/64/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/64> ;
      zoo:text "And the merlin is enemy to the fox, but the raven and the fox are friends – the raven, too, makes war on the merlin, which is why it comes to the aid of the fox when the latter is struck." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/68/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/68> ;
      zoo:text "Of those animals that have bladders, all have intestines as well, but of those that have intestines, ⟨not⟩ all have bladders." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/90/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/90> ;
      zoo:text "In the snow there are shaggy worm-like creatures. In Cyprus, where copper ore is smelted, a creature slightly larger than a fly is generated: the same thing happens in the furnaces at Carystus. These animals die when separated from snow and fire, respectively." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/60/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/60> ;
      zoo:text "Of marine creatures, the dolphin is the tamest – indeed, they behave in a passionate manner towards boys, as has happened around Tarentum, Caria and many other places. In Caria, when a dolphin had been captured and inflicted with many wounds, a large number of them came into the harbour to its aid until the fisherman released it." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/111/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/111> ;
      zoo:text "Goats and sheep give birth, for the most part, to male offspring if they copulate while there is a north wind, but to female in the case of a south wind. The offspring are white if there are white veins under the tongue of the ram, but black if the veins are black. The same principle holds for red offspring. ⟨Those⟩ that drink salted water are the first to be mounted." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/23/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/23> ;
      zoo:text "There are singular things, too, concerning the formation and transformations of animals, particularly concerning their genesis. Thus, in Egypt if an ox is buried in a certain place so that the horns protrude above the earth and are afterwards sawn off, they say that bees fly out of them: for in the course of rotting the ox is dissolved into this insect. And Philetas, who is pretty inquisitive, seems to turn his attention to this; so he addresses the bees as 'ox-born' when he says: 'With long strides first you reached the ox-born bees' (a) They say, too, that the crocodile generates scorpions, (b) and that wasps are generated from horses. And a certain Archelaus, an Egyptian who interpreted paradoxa for Ptolemy in epigrams, spoke thus concerning scorpions: (a) Into you, scorpions, does nature who gives life to all things dissolve the crocodile when it has died. And about wasps: (b) Note this offspring of a horse’s corpse: wasps – behold what nature creates from what! Aristotle says that scorpions are generated from rotting bergamot-mint." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/8/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/8> ;
      zoo:text "Something no less marvellous than this, but much more familiar, is the fact about the thorn in Sicily called cardoon: when a deer treads on it and is injured, its bones are soundless and useless for the making of flutes. And from this Philetas expounded about [the deer], when he said: 'The fawn will sing after its departure from life if it has guarded itself from the prick of the sharp cardoon'" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/38/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/38> ;
      zoo:text "The Egyptian mongoose, whenever it sees an asp, does not attack it before calling upon others to help. They plaster themselves in mud as a protection against its bites and blows: first they moisten their skin, then they roll about in the dust." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/55/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/55> ;
      zoo:text "The octopus puts food into its den and whenever the usable bits are consumed, it expels the unusable and preys on those little fish which come to feed on the cast-offs by changing its colour to closely resemble that of the stones it happens to be nearby. It does the same thing whenever it is frightened." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/43/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/43> ;
      zoo:text "Aristotle says that the swallow, when building its nest, plaits mud together with twigs. If mud is lacking, it gets itself wet and rolls about and picks it up with its wings, and makes itself a bed of straw and leaves using the same techniques as a human being, putting hard material underneath. It gives its young their food in turn, watching carefully lest it feed the same ones twice. When they are small, it throws their excrement out itself, but when they have grown, it teaches the young to turn around and discharge the excrement outside." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/109/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/109> ;
      zoo:text "No-one has seen the chicks of the cuckoo, for it does not lay its eggs in its own nest but flies into those of small birds, either those of the wild pigeon or those of the hypolaïs, after it has consumed the eggs already there." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/126/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/126> ;
      zoo:text "If the corner of the eye is fleshy, it indicates a dishonest nature; middling ears are indicative of the finest character, but those which are large and protruding, of foolish talk and prattling." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/88/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/88> ;
      zoo:text "The so-called starfish is so hot that whatever fish it catches hold of is at once boiled." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/40/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/40> ;
      zoo:text "The tortoise, whenever it eats a snake, eats oregano as an antidote. Someone once observed this closely and stripped the plant of its leaves: the tortoise died since it did not have oregano to eat." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/85/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/85> ;
      zoo:text "He says that ants, when sprinkled with oregano and sulphur, abandon their anthill." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/59/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/59> ;
      zoo:text "They say that the King of Scythia had a noble mare: he led a foal of her own to her so that it might mate, but the foal was unwilling. When they put a covering over the mare and then led her in, the foal mounted her; but when she had been unveiled and he saw her face, he fled and threw himself off a cliff." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/14/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/14> ;
      zoo:text "Theopompus says that there is in the neighbourhood of the Chalcidians in Thrace a place of the following nature. Whenever any other sort of animal enters it, it comes back unharmed, but no dung-beetle escapes: it turns round and round in a circle and then dies. Indeed, the place is called Cantharolethrus (‘Beetle-death’) because of this." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/47/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/47> ;
      zoo:text "He relates that pelicans swallow down ⟨large⟩ smooth mussels. They hold them down for a while in the crop above their stomachs, then aftwerward – when the shells have opened – regurgitate them and eat the meat now that they have separated it in this way." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/102/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/102> ;
      zoo:text "Every creature bitten by a rabid dog becomes rabid, except humans." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/52/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/52> ;
      zoo:text "Aristotle says that when the eagle grows old, its beak grows and curves and it finally dies of starvation. The vulture takes the young cast out by the eagle and brings them up. The ⟨sea⟩-eagle compels its young while still unfeathered to look towards the sun, and whichever one of them gets watery eyes and is unwilling to face the sun, it kills." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/81/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/81> ;
      zoo:text "In Phrygia there are cattle which can move their horns." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/1/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANTIGONUS/collection_of_marvellous_accounts/e/1/1> ;
      zoo:text "Timaeus, who wrote the history of Sicily, says that where the river called Alex forms a boundary between the Locrians and the people of Rhegium, the cicadas on the Locrian side sing while those on the Rhegine side are voiceless. But something yet more fabulous than this is told: for when the citharodes Ariston from Rhegium and Eunomus from Locri arrived at Delphi, they disagreed between themselves about their allotted order of performance – the former thought that he ought not to draw the inferior position since the entire colony of the Rhegines had come from Delphi and from the command of the god, while the latter inveighed against him that it was not at all fitting for those to sing to the cithara among whom even the cicadas did not sing. So the Rhegine was set fair for success, but Eunomus the Locrian was victorious in the contest for this reason: while he was singing, a cicada which had flown onto his cithara began to sing, and the assembled multitude acclaimed the occurrence and demanded his victory." .


